```

### 4. Launch eww

```bash
eww daemon
eww open sidebar-window
eww open sysmonitor-window
eww open clock-window
```

### 5. Autostart with i3

Add to `~/.config/i3/config`:
```
exec --no-startup-id eww daemon
exec --no-startup-id eww open sidebar-window
exec --no-startup-id eww open sysmonitor-window
exec --no-startup-id eww open clock-window
exec --no-startup-id picom
exec --no-startup-id feh --bg-fill ~/Pictures/wall.jpg
```

Reload i3 (`Mod+Shift+R`) and paste back what you see â€” positions/sizes are easy to tweak once we see it rendered.

Great â€” the widgets are live and rendering exactly where they should. "Liquid Glass" (translucent, blurred, glossy) is achievable, but it's two-part: the **CSS gives the glass look**, and **picom gives the actual background blur** (GTK CSS itself can't blur what's behind it).

### 1. Enable blur in picom

Edit/create `~/.config/picom/picom.conf`:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/.config/picom
nano ~/.config/picom/picom.conf
```

Add:
```
backend = "glx";
blur-method = "dual_kawase";
blur-strength = 8;

blur-background-exclude = [
  "class_g = 'slop'"
];

corner-radius = 16;
rounded-corners-exclude = [];

shadow = true;
shadow-radius = 20;
shadow-opacity = 0.4;

fading = true;
fade-in-step = 0.03;
fade-out-step = 0.03;
```

Restart picom:
```bash
pkill picom
picom &
```

### 2. Update `~/.config/eww/eww.scss` for the glass look

```scss
* {
  font-family: "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font", monospace;
}

.sidebar {
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
  border-radius: 20px;
  padding: 14px;
  box-shadow: 0px 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
}

.launcher-item {
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
  color: #f5eaea;
  padding: 10px 12px;
  margin: 5px 0px;
  border-radius: 14px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
  transition: all 0.2s ease;
}

.launcher-item:hover {
  background-color: rgba(217, 122, 122, 0.25);
  border: 1px solid rgba(217, 122, 122, 0.4);
}

.launcher-icon {
  margin-right: 10px;
  color: #e39a9a;
}

.sys-box {
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
  border-radius: 18px;
  padding: 14px;
  color: #f5eaea;
  box-shadow: 0px 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
}

.sys-title {
  color: #e39a9a;
  font-size: 11px;
  margin-bottom: 8px;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
}

.sys-row {
  margin: 3px 0px;
}

.sys-val {
  color: #ffffff;
  margin-left: 12px;
}

.clock-box {
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
  border-radius: 18px;
  padding: 14px;
  color: #f5eaea;
  box-shadow: 0px 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
}

.clock-time {
  font-size: 28px;
  color: #ffffff;
  text-shadow: 0px 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
}

.clock-date {
  font-size: 12px;
  color: #e39a9a;
}
```

Reload eww's CSS (no restart needed):
```bash
eww reload
```

**Important caveat:** GTK's rendering under `vmwgfx`/software rasterizing might not do real-time blur smoothly since it's compositor-dependent â€” if picom's `glx` backend fails on this VM (given your earlier vmwgfx crash), switch to:
```
backend = "xrender";
```
in picom.conf (no blur support, but stable) and rely on the semi-transparent layered CSS above for the glass *look* without true blur.

Try `eww reload` first and tell me what you see â€” if picom's glx backend crashes again like before, we fall back to xrender immediately.
